Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; fogbound Victorian London; candlelight, frost, and chain-clank.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Fog hangs thick over the crowded paths. A sign above a warehouse door reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, thin-lipped, blue-chinned, sharp-nosed, in a dark great-coat, edges through the crowd; beggars and children give him wide berth. Blind men's dogs tug their owners into doorways.

(SOUND: distant church bells muffled in fog, boots on wet stone, a cough in the cold)

NARRATOR
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail. Scrooge never painted out Old Marley's name. Sometimes people new to the business called Scrooge Scrooge, and sometimes Marley, but he answered to both names. Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grind-stone, Scrooge! a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous, old sinner!

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A dismal office. Scrooge's small fire barely glows. Beyond, in a little cell like a tank, BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare, white comforter about his neck, copies letters at a desk, trying to warm his hands at a single candle. The coal-box sits by Scrooge.

(SOUND: scratch of quill, fog-drip at the keyhole, city clocks striking three)

The door bursts open. FRED, Scrooge's nephew, ruddy, handsome, glowing from the cold, strides in.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew, keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
Keep it! But you don't keep it. I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; and therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes out his last frail spark of fire.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation! You're quite a powerful speaker, sir. I wonder you don't go into Parliament.

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without an angry word. At the outer door he greets Bob cordially. Two portly GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute, who suffer greatly at the present time. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. Since you ask me what I wish, gentlemen, that is my answer. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

GENTLEMAN
They are. Still. I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The Gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his labours, improved in temper.

EXT. COUNTING-HOUSE DOOR - LATER

Fog thicker, colder. A thin CAROL BOY stoops at the keyhole.

(SOUND: thin boyish voice starting a carol)

CAROL BOY
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes his ruler. The boy flees into the frost.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - EVENING

Shutting-up hour. Bob snuffs his candle and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge growls out. Bob, long ends of his white comforter dangling, runs off toward Camden Town.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang about the black old gateway. Scrooge, key in hand, approaches the heavy door. The knocker is very large—and becomes MARLEY'S FACE: livid, spectacles on ghostly forehead, hair stirred as by hot air, eyes wide and motionless.

(SOUND: wind in the yard, a low unearthly hum)

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key sturdily, lights his candle, looks behind the door—nothing but screws—and bangs it shut.

(SOUND: door-bang like thunder, echoes through empty rooms and cellars)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. Small fire, spoon and basin, little saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, in dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, double-locks himself in and sits to take his gruel. Dutch tiles around the old fireplace. Marley's face seems to swim on every one.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He paces, sits again. A disused bell begins to swing—softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers.

(SOUND: bells rising together, then ceasing; a clanking deep below, chain dragged over casks)

The cellar-door booms. The noise climbs the stairs, comes straight toward his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without a pause the shape passes through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up.

MARLEY'S GHOST stands before him: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots; a long chain clasped about his middle, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses wrought in steel. His body is transparent. A folded kerchief binds his head and chin.

(SOUND: chain links settling; the fire's brief cry and fall)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite the fireplace.

MARLEY'S GHOST
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. Scrooge clings to his chair. The phantom takes off the bandage; its lower jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls upon his knees.

(SOUND: appalling chain-shake; jaw-drop click; Scrooge's gasp)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. In life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me! Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business. Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds the wrapper round its head again. It walks backward; the window raises itself. Confused noises of lamentation fill the air. Marley's Ghost floats out upon the bleak dark night. Outside, phantoms in chains wander and moan—one old ghost with an iron safe at its ankle cries piteously, unable to help a woman with an infant on a door-step.

(SOUND: mournful dirge of spirits; wind; fading wails)

Scrooge closes the window. The door is double-locked still. He tries to say "Humbug," stops at the first syllable, and falls asleep upon the instant.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Darkness. Church chimes strike the quarters, then a deep, dull, hollow, melancholy ONE. Light flashes. The bed-curtains are drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: deep ONE of the bell; rush of strange light)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands close: like a child yet like an old man; white hair, unwrinkled face, tunic of purest white, lustrous belt, branch of fresh green holly, dress trimmed with summer flowers; from its head a bright clear jet of light; a great extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure fluctuates—now one arm, now twenty legs—then is itself again.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Clear cold winter day, snow on the ground. Fields on either hand. A little market-town in the distance. Boys on shaggy ponies shout Merry Christmas.

(SOUND: crisp air, merry shouts, ponies' hooves)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us. The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL ROOM - DAY

A long bare melancholy room. At a desk by a feeble fire, YOUNG SCROOGE, a lonely boy, reads. Adult Scrooge sits upon a form and weeps.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from a water-spout, idle swing of a door)

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

A little girl, FAN, much younger, darts in, arms about the boy's neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! and are never to come back here; but first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart! She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Old FEZZIWIG in a Welsh wig at a high desk. The clock points to seven. YOUNG SCROOGE, now a young man, and DICK WILKINS hurry in.

(SOUND: lively fiddle tuning; laughter; shutters slammed up)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

The warehouse becomes a snug ball-room. In come Mrs. Fezziwig, the three Miss Fezziwigs, apprentices, housemaid, cook, milkman—twenty couple at once. Fezziwig dances with wonderful agility; a positive light seems to issue from his calves. At eleven the ball breaks up; Fezziwig and his wife shake hands with every person and wish them a Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. CITY STREET / INT. BELLE'S ROOM - EVENING

Young Scrooge, older now, face beginning to wear care and avarice, sits beside BELLE, a fair young girl in mourning-dress, tears in her eyes.

(SOUND: muted street tumult under snow; soft crackle of a small fire)

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. Another shadow: Belle, now a comely matron, with daughter and a tumult of children; her husband enters laden with Christmas toys. He mentions having seen Scrooge alone in his office, partner dying.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me? Remove me! I cannot bear it!

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Spirit's head. Light streams from under it. Scrooge reels to bed and sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

The bell upon the stroke of One. A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. Scrooge shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls him by name.

(SOUND: the ONE; roar of a mighty blaze; strange cheerful voice)

The room is hung with living green—holly, mistletoe, ivy. Heaped on the floor: turkeys, geese, game, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, barrels of oysters, seething bowls of punch. Upon this throne sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a jolly Giant in a green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast, bare feet, holly wreath with icicles, glowing torch like Plenty's horn.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow on roofs, people scraping pavements, poulterers' and fruiterers' radiant, church bells calling. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch on poor dinners carried to the bakers; angry words turn to good humour.

(SOUND: bells, scrapers on stone, cheerful street bustle, torch-hiss)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

A four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in ribbons on a twice-turned gown, lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER plunges a fork into potatoes. The two young Cratchits dance about. MARTHA arrives; they hide her as BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, iron frame on his limbs.

(SOUND: pudding singing in the copper; crutch on floorboards; family chatter)

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tiny Tim off to hear the pudding.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

They fetch the goose. Grace. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the knife; stuffing issues forth; Tiny Tim beats on the table and cries Hurrah. The pudding arrives like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in ignited brandy, holly on top. Later, chestnuts on the fire; Bob serves the hot mixture from a jug in two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle.

(SOUND: hiss of gravy, crackle of chestnuts, soft family laughter)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Bob holds Tim's withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

BOB CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. It should be Christmas Day, I am sure, on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The shadow of Scrooge's name passes. They grow merrier. Tiny Tim sings a plaintive little song. Scrooge's eye stays on him until the last.

INT. FRED'S DRAWING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright dry gleaming room. FRED laughs heartily—holding his sides. His pretty NIECE and friends roar with him. Topper eyes the plump sister.

(SOUND: contagious laughter, harp, clink of glasses)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too! He's a comical old fellow, and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it—I defy him—if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Uncle Scrooge! A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is!

Scrooge, unseen, would have pledged them if the Spirit had given him time.

EXT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown old; hair grey. From its robe it brings two children: wretched, abject, yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish—a boy and a girl.

(SOUND: midnight wind; distant chimes beginning)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone. A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like a mist along the ground.

EXT. CITY / 'CHANGE - DAY (YET TO COME)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME—deep black garment, nothing visible save one outstretched hand—points onward. Scrooge follows, trembling.

(SOUND: chink of money in pockets; hurried merchant voices; then hush)

MR. BARKER
I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. LEECH
When did he die?

MR. BARKER
Last night, I believe. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral; for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

Another pair:

MR. CHAPMAN
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. GROSVENOR
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

A low-browed shop of iron, rags, bottles, bones. OLD JOE by a charcoal stove. CHARWOMAN, MRS. DILBER the laundress, and UNDERTAKER'S MAN enter with bundles; they burst into a laugh.

(SOUND: rusty hinge shriek; chalk on wall; coarse laughter)

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? Open that bundle, old Joe.

They produce seal, pencil-case, sheets, towels, sugar-tongs—and bed-curtains, rings and all; blankets; a fine shirt taken off the corpse.

CHARWOMAN
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own.

The scene changes: a bare uncurtained bed; beneath a ragged sheet, a body, plundered, unwatched, unwept. A cat tears at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearth-stone.

(SOUND: cat at the door; gnawing rats; terrible stillness)

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' HOME - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, and children. Her husband enters, careworn yet with a serious shameful delight.

HUSBAND
Bad. We are not ruined. There is hope yet, Caroline. He is past relenting. He is dead. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Their hearts are lighter. The only emotion the Ghost can show is pleasure.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY (FUTURE)

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and the daughters sew. Peter reads. The noisy little Cratchits are still as statues.

(SOUND: soft fire; a single caught breath)

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world.

PETER
I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble.

Bob enters, comforter on. He is cheerful, then breaks.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs, kisses the little face in the room hung with Christmas, returns. He tells of Fred's kindness in the street.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim—shall we—or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Walled in by houses; grass and weeds; choked with too much burying. The Spirit points down to one neglected grave. Scrooge advances, trembling.

(SOUND: wind in weeds; far bell; Scrooge's ragged breath)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only? Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable. Scrooge reads upon the stone: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope! Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He catches the spectral hand. The Phantom repulses him. The hood and dress shrink, collapse, dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed is his own. The room is his own. Time before him is his own. Scrooge scrambles out, laughing and crying, stockings in a tangle.

(SOUND: lustiest church peals—clash, clang, hammer, ding, dong, bell)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees! They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here—I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been, may be dispelled. I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window, opens it. No fog. Clear, bright, jovial cold. Golden sunlight. A BOY in Sunday clothes below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
EH?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge, dressed in his best, regards everyone with a delighted smile. Three good-humoured fellows wish him merry Christmas. He meets the portly Gentleman of the day before.

(SOUND: bright street noise, footsteps, cheerful greetings)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the Gentleman's ear.

GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - AFTERNOON

Table spread in great array. Scrooge sidles his face round the door.

(SOUND: warm room murmur; a startled chair-scrape)

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?—Let him in!

Hearty shaking of hands. Wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half behind, plunges to his stool.

(SOUND: clock strike; hurried pen; Scrooge's feigned growl)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

INT. CRATCHIT HOME / EXT. LONDON - MONTAGE - DAY

Scrooge at the Cratchits' fire, a second father to Tiny Tim, who does NOT die. Scrooge as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. Some laugh at the alteration; his own heart laughs, and that is quite enough.

(MUSIC: warm swelling carol theme, gentle and resolved)

(SOUND: Tiny Tim's little crutch, then laughter; distant happy bells)

NARRATOR
He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed—

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
